⚠ PRE-FLIGHT REQUIRED before any code (9-point — extended Batch 15→16 lesson preserved):
1. Run `git status` + `git log -1` to confirm working tree state matches expectations.
2. Confirm `pnpm vitest run` → 656/656 GREEN baseline (or document deviation if no source
   drift since last GREEN, like Batches 13/14/15/16 did).
3. Read the input Zod schema of any router procedure that .whatsnext claims will be reused —
   this catches scope mismatches BEFORE the BUILD BATCH PROPOSAL stage (Batch 12 lesson).
4. If a router change in scope newly READS a field from the existing record (e.g. a gate or
   conditional), verify all tests hitting that code path mock that field (Batch 13 lesson).
5. Grep schema.prisma for any model referenced in the proposed scope; verify presence and
   field types before scoping migrations. (Batch 14+15+16 each paid for itself here — every
   "new entity" turned out to already be in the init migration.)
6. Confirm package.json contains any third-party library called out in the proposed scope.
   ⚠ Specifically for Xendit: `grep -E "xendit|stripe" apps/web/package.json` — NOT present
   as of Batch 16 close. Any checkout work must install `xendit-node` as Item 0.
7. Re-read .claude/rules/memory-governance.md §1 (Tiered Decomposition) for this batch's tier.
8. ⚠ Emit explicit branch-creation line item in the BUILD BATCH PROPOSAL so user can catch
   silent direct-to-main slips at confirm time. Example:
   "Branch feat/batch-17-item-1-{slug} will be created before any file write."
9. ⚠ When proposal text says "Server Component consuming X.Y procedure" for a PUBLIC route,
   grep the router for `X.Y: publicProcedure` vs `protectedProcedure`. Mismatch means either
   (a) pivot to direct Prisma in the page, or (b) split the procedure into public + protected
   variants. Pre-flight item 9 paid off in Batch 16 — zero pivots during execution because
   the check ran at scope time.

